    "status": "working-edition",
    "notes": "The prayer over the water poured into the chalice, said while the water is blessed — hence its own document, and hence the second rubric, which records that in Masses for the Dead the prayer is said but the water is not blessed. The demonstrative hic, hæc, hoc carries the plain lemma key hic — SCHEMA.md gives the bare key to the word a reader is likelier to look up — while the adverb lives under hic_adverbium (so at ordinarium.aufer-a-nobis w031). Discriminated keys map back through LEMMA_ALIASES, and the analyzers vote on both. FÍERI at w026 is a standing divergence: Collatinus reads the form as an imperative, where it is the present infinitive of fio, as the lexicon's own dictionary head (fio, fíeri, factus sum) and Whitaker's have it; the token's provenance records the disagreement rather than hiding it. MIRABÍLIUS at w009 is the comparative of the adverb mirabíliter (w006), not the neuter comparative of the adjective: only Whitaker's carries the adverbial reading, Collatinus offers the adjective, and the corpus follows the syntax — the two words are parallel and both modify verbs. HUIUS at w014 carries no gender: it qualifies aquæ (feminine) and vini (neuter) together and its own form marks none. EIUS at w019 is masculine by its referent, Iesus Christus, named eleven words later; the form marks no gender. CONSÓRTES at w022 is accusative, not nominative — the form is both — because the understood subject of esse is an accusative us, not the dative nobis.",
